---Request-level diagnostics: why the prompt cache could not fully reuse
---the prefix of the request named by `diagnostics.previous_message_id`.
---@class anthropic.beta_messages.BetaDiagnostics
---@field cache_miss_reason anthropic.beta_messages.BetaCacheMissReason|distilled.Null Explains why the prompt cache could not fully reuse the prefix from the request identified by `diagnostics.previous_message_id`. `null` means diagnosis is still pending — the response was serialized before the background comparison completed.

---Request-level diagnostics. Currently carries the previous response
---id for prompt-cache divergence reporting.
---@class anthropic.beta_messages.BetaDiagnosticsParam
---@field previous_message_id? string|distilled.Null The `id` (`msg_...`) from this client's previous /v1/messages response. The server compares that request's prompt fingerprint against this one and returns `diagnostics.cache_miss_reason` when the prompt-cache prefix could not be reused. Pass `null` on the first turn to opt in without a prior message to compare.

---Tool invocation directly from the model.
---@class anthropic.beta_messages.BetaDirectCaller
---@field type string

---All possible effort levels.
---@alias anthropic.beta_messages.BetaEffortLevel "low"|"medium"|"high"|"xhigh"|"max"

---One entry in the `fallbacks` chain on a `/v1/messages` request.
---
---`model` is required. The override fields (`max_tokens`, `thinking`,
---`output_config`, and `speed`) set the corresponding parameter for this
---attempt only and are validated as if the request were made to `model`.
---Any other key is rejected at parse time.
---@class anthropic.beta_messages.BetaFallbackConfigV2
---@field max_tokens? number|distilled.Null The `max_tokens` for the fallback attempt that uses this `fallbacks` entry's `model`, in place of the request's `max_tokens`. If you leave it out or set it to `null`, the attempt uses the request's `max_tokens`. See the [refusals and fallback guide](https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/refusals-and-fallback#naming-your-own-fallback-models) to learn more about fallback entries.
---@field model anthropic.beta_messages.Model
---@field output_config? anthropic.beta_messages.BetaOutputConfig|distilled.Null Output parameters for the fallback attempt that uses this `fallbacks` entry's `model`, combined with the request's `output_config` one field at a time. - `format`: If you leave it out, the attempt uses the request's `format`. If you set it to `null`, the attempt runs without a format. - `effort`: If you leave it out, the attempt uses the request's `effort` when `model` supports that level with the attempt's `thinking`. If the request doesn't set `effort`, if `model` doesn't support the level, or if you set `effort` to `null`, the attempt runs at the default effort of `model`. - `task_budget`: If you leave it out, the attempt uses the request's `task_budget` when `model` accepts a task budget of that size. If the request doesn't set `task_budget`, if `model` doesn't accept it, or if you set `task_budget` to `null`, the attempt runs without a task budget. Set `output_config` to `null` to run the attempt without a format or a task budget, at the default effort of `model`. These rules apply with the `server-side-fallback-2026-07-01` beta header.
---@field speed? anthropic.beta_messages.BetaSpeed|string|distilled.Null The inference speed mode for the fallback attempt that uses this `fallbacks` entry's `model`. If you leave it out, the attempt runs at `fast` speed only when the request sets `speed` to `fast` and your organization can use fast mode on `model`. Otherwise, or when you set this field to `null`, the attempt runs at `standard` speed. These rules apply with the `server-side-fallback-2026-07-01` beta header.
---@field thinking? anthropic.beta_messages.BetaFallbackConfigV2Thinking|distilled.Null The thinking configuration for the fallback attempt that uses this `fallbacks` entry's `model`. If you leave it out, the attempt uses the request's `thinking` when `model` supports that thinking `type`. If you leave it out and the request's thinking `type` is `between_tools` but `model` doesn't support `between_tools`, the attempt uses `disabled` if `model` supports it. Otherwise, if `model` doesn't support the request's thinking `type`, if the request doesn't set `thinking`, or if you set this field to `null`, the attempt uses the thinking `type` that `model` uses when a request leaves out `thinking`. The request's `thinking.display` still applies where `model` allows it. These rules apply with the `server-side-fallback-2026-07-01` beta header.
---@field [string] any

---@alias anthropic.beta_messages.BetaFallbackConfigV2Thinking anthropic.beta_messages.BetaThinkingConfigEnabled|anthropic.beta_messages.BetaThinkingConfigDisabled|anthropic.beta_messages.BetaThinkingConfigBetweenTools|anthropic.beta_messages.BetaThinkingConfigAdaptive

